## A — closed, and closed at the root

`:303-320` replaces interpolation with a measured three-row `code` table, and
steps 1 (`:329-338`) and 4 (`:361-369`) switch on `code`, never on `error`.
I re-measured every wording the two new sites can produce against 7(k)'s three
assertions (`probe-7k.mts`):

```
len= 42 a1=true a3-clean=true  served-e2e screenshot board.png is missing
len= 98 a1=true a3-clean=true  …is unsafe or unreadable: path reaches its target through a symlink
len= 85 a1=true a3-clean=true  …is unsafe or unreadable: target exceeds 4194304 bytes
len= 81 a1=true a3-clean=true  …manifest is unsafe or unreadable: target exceeds 8192 bytes
len= 72 a1=true a3-clean=true  …manifest needs an opus id of at most 22 characters
len=122 a1=true a3-clean=true  …is unsafe or unreadable: path must be a nonempty officina-relative string
```

The worst case is **122** characters against a 160 cap, all printable ASCII,
none carrying a path. 7(b)'s Genuine red now produces
`served-e2e screenshot board.png is missing` — 42 characters, and deliberately
free of `/unsafe|unreadable|symlink|regular file/i`, so it is one finding and
7(b) and 7(k) no longer contradict. The reasoning at `:363-365` ("a deleted
file is a gate problem and not a containment event") is the right distinction
to draw, and it is the one that makes the count fall out rather than being
asserted.

The double-raise set is now exactly right, and I checked it against the real
`addPolicyProblem` (`opus-model.ts:784-787`) rather than the brief's summary:
of the helper's seven fixed refusals, only `path reaches its target through a
symlink` and `target is not a regular file` carry a matching substring on
their own — but because the `code === undefined` branch wraps all of them in
`is unsafe or unreadable:`, every refusal double-raises uniformly. That is why
7(d) (symlink, both cases), 7(i)'s second half (`target exceeds`) and 7(j)
(`target exceeds` at step 1) are the three two-finding rows and nothing else
is. I traced both of 7(d)'s cases through the helper's component loop
(`opus-model.ts:218-222`): a symlinked parent directory is caught at the parent
component and yields the same symlink refusal, not an escape refusal, so both
halves of (d) are two findings as stated.

The nine leaky precedent sites are enumerated at `:316-317`
(`opus-model.ts:583, 598, 621, 637, 675, 696, 727, 890, 939` — matching my own
search exactly), two owned, the rest filed, and `:315-318` says plainly that
the precedent is leaky and these two are a deliberate improvement on it. That
is the right scope: W-110 takes its own, and does not widen.

