## SHOULD (1, non-blocking)

**7(k)'s third assertion is a near-no-op against the leak shape it names.**
`:813` writes "contains no absolute path (no message matches `/(?:^|\s)\//`)".
Measured against the exact string revision 2 would have produced:

```
revision-2 leak string, length: 157
  assertion 1 (ASCII<=160) catches it? false
  assertion 2 (/tmp substring) catches it? true
  assertion 3 (no absolute path) catches it? false   <-- the / follows a quote, not whitespace
```

Node renders the path quoted — `… directory, lstat '/tmp/claude-1000/…'` — so
the `/` is preceded by `'`, not by whitespace or start-of-string, and the
assertion named "no absolute path" does not fire. The 157-character length
also slips under the 160 cap, so assertion 1 does not fire either. **The row is
still a sound guard**, because assertion 2's first clause (the test's own
temp-directory path, which is the officina root and therefore a prefix of any
leaked path) catches it on every platform — so nothing fails and this is not a
MUST. But the clause advertised as the absolute-path guard should actually be
one: `/\/[\w.-]+\//` (two separators with a segment between) catches
`/tmp/claude-1000` and macOS's `/var/folders/…` while still allowing the
closed-set refusals `path must remain under ci/` and `real parent escapes ci/`
— I verified all seven refusal strings pass that form. Either tighten it or
drop it and let assertion 2 carry the row, but do not leave a clause that
reads like a guarantee it does not give.

## Residual note (not a finding)

`:312-314` treats `code === undefined` as equivalent to "one of the helper's
own fixed refusals". That holds today for a good reason — the helper's own
refusals `return { error }` with no `code`, and every throw inside its `try`
comes from `realpathSync`/`lstatSync`/`openSync`/`fstatSync`/`readFileSync`,
all of which carry `.code` (`opus-model.ts:239-241` only attaches `code` when
the thrown value has one). A non-errno throw would land in that branch and have
its message interpolated. It is practically unreachable, and no row in
behaviours 6 or 7 can reach it, so it does not change the verdict — but since
Interfaces already enumerates the closed set of seven, asserting membership and
falling back to the bare-code branch otherwise would make the property
structural rather than inferred, which is the same move the 22-character cap
made for `redact` in revision 2.

